9.theory:
## Classic switch

```java
int day = 3;
switch (day) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Mon");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Tue");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Other");
}
```

- `switch (expression)` — only `int`, `char`, `String`, `enum`.
- `break` is mandatory, otherwise fall-through into the next `case`.
- `default` — when no `case` matched.

## Fall-through

```java
switch (x) {
    case 1:
    case 2:
        System.out.println("1 or 2");
        break;
}
```

Empty `case` labels group together.

## New switch (Java 14+)

```java
String result = switch (day) {
    case 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 -> "weekday";
    case 6, 7 -> "weekend";
    default -> "?";
};
```

`->` without `break`. Requires a modern JDK.

## switch vs if

`switch` is good for **exact matches** against a few values. `if` is for ranges.

## What if

- Forget `break` — falls through into later `case` blocks.
- `switch` on `double` — compile error.
- No `default` and no match — nothing executes.

## Common mistake

Forgetting `break` in classic `switch` — the program runs extra `case` blocks.

## Summary

- `switch (expression) { case X: ... break; }`
- Only `int`, `char`, `String`, `enum`
- `break` — otherwise fall-through
- `default` — otherwise nothing
- Java 14+: `case X -> ...` without break

