10.theory:
## for

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

Three parts separated by `;`: initialization, condition, step.

## while

```java
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}
```

Check **before** the body. If the condition is immediately false — the body runs zero times.

## do-while

```java
int i = 1;
do {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
} while (i <= 5);
```

Check **after** the body. Runs at least once. `while (...)` **with `;`**.

## break / continue

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    if (i == 5) break;         // exit loop
    if (i % 2 == 0) continue;  // skip iteration
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

## Nested

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 2; j++) {
        System.out.println(i + "," + j);
    }
}
```

The inner loop runs fully on each outer iteration.

## What if

- Forget `i++` — infinite loop.
- Forget `;` after `do { } while (...)` — compile error.
- `continue` inside `switch` inside `for` — works, but easy to get confused.

## Common mistake

Infinite `while` when you forget to update the variable. The program hangs, only `Ctrl+C` helps.

## Summary

- `for (init; cond; step)` — known iteration count
- `while (cond)` — by condition
- `do { } while (cond);` — at least once
- `break` — exit, `continue` — skip iteration
- Without update — infinite loop

