12.theory:
## Classic for

```java
int[] arr = {10, 20, 30};
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(arr[i]);
}
```

Has an index `i`. Useful when you need the number or want to modify elements.

## for-each

```java
for (int x : arr) {
    System.out.println(x);
}
```

Shorter. But **no index** and **no modification** (x is a copy). Ideal for reading.

## Sum

```java
int sum = 0;
for (int x : arr) sum += x;
System.out.println(sum);
```

Accumulator `sum` **outside** the loop. Inside — resets every iteration.

## Max

```java
int max = arr[0];
for (int x : arr) {
    if (x > max) max = x;
}
```

Start from `arr[0]`. From `0` — for all-negative arrays the max would be `0`, not the real one.

## Search

```java
int target = 20;
for (int x : arr) {
    if (x == target) {
        System.out.println("found");
        break;
    }
}
```

## Modifying — only via index

```java
for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) arr[i] *= 2;
```

for-each cannot — `x` is a copy.

## What if

- Sum declared inside the loop — resets.
- Max starting from zero — wrong for negative arrays.
- for-each with assignment — changes are lost.

## Common mistake

`for (int x : arr) { x = x * 2; }` — you thought you doubled the array. But `x` is a copy. For modification use classic `for`.

## Summary

- `for (i...)` — with index
- `for (x : arr)` — without, for reading
- Accumulator — before the loop
- Max — from `arr[0]`
- Modify — via `for` with index

