14.theory:
## Why classes

`int`, `String` — built-in types. A class is a **custom** type: data + behavior in one place.

```java
class Person {
    String name;
    int age;

    void sayHi() {
        System.out.println("Hi, " + name);
    }
}
```

`class Person` — a blueprint. Until an instance is created, nothing exists in memory.

## Creating an object

```java
Person p = new Person();
p.name = "Anna";
p.age = 25;
p.sayHi(); // Hi, Anna
```

`new Person()` — create an object. `p` — a reference. `p.name` — field access.

## Difference from Python

In Python `p = Person()` without `new`, fields created dynamically. In Java — `new`, fields **declared** in the class.

## Several objects

```java
Person a = new Person();
Person b = new Person();
a.name = "Anna";
b.name = "Ivan";
a.sayHi(); // Hi, Anna
b.sayHi(); // Hi, Ivan
```

Each object has its own fields. Methods are shared.

## Where to declare

One `public class` per file. `Person` without `public` — same file:

```java
class Person { }

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Person p = new Person();
    }
}
```

## What if

- `Person p;` without `new`, then `p.name` — `NullPointerException`.
- Two `public class` in one file — compile error.
- Access a field that does not exist — compile error.

## Common mistake

`Person p = new Person();` then `p = null; p.sayHi()` — NPE. Check that the object exists.

## Summary

- `class Name { fields; methods; }`
- `new Name()` — create object
- `p.field` — access
- Each object has its own fields
- `public class` — one per file

