15.theory:
## The problem

```java
Person p = new Person();
p.name = "Anna";
p.age = 25;
```

Three lines just to initialize. Forget a field — silently stays `null` or `0`. A constructor fixes it.

## Constructor

```java
class Person {
    String name;
    int age;

    Person(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}
```

A method without a return type, name equals the class name. Called via `new`:

```java
Person p = new Person("Anna", 25);
```

## this

`this.name` — the object's field, `name` — the parameter. Without `this`, `name = name` does nothing.

## Default constructor

If you declare **no constructor**, Java creates an empty one. Declare **any** — the empty one **disappears**. `new Person()` stops compiling.

## Overloading

```java
Person(String name) { this(name, 0); }
Person(String name, int age) { this.name = name; this.age = age; }
```

`this(...)` — call **another** constructor. Only on the **first line**.

## What if

- Forget `this` — fields not set.
- Declare a parameterized constructor and call `new Person()` — compile error.
- `this(...)` not on the first line — compile error.

## Common mistake

`name = name;` in the constructor — the parameter assigns to itself. The field never changes. Need `this.name = name;`.

## Summary

- `ClassName(params)` — constructor
- Called via `new`
- `this.field = param` — assign
- Parameterized constructor → no empty one
- `this(...)` — call another constructor

