16.theory:
## The problem

```java
Person p = new Person("Anna", 25);
p.age = -100; // nonsense, but the compiler allows it
```

Fields without a modifier are visible to everyone. Anyone can break the state.

## private

```java
class Person {
    private String name;
    private int age;
}
```

`p.age` from **outside** — compile error. Only methods of the class can touch it.

## Getters/setters

```java
class Person {
    private int age;
    public int getAge() { return age; }
    public void setAge(int age) {
        if (age >= 0) this.age = age;
    }
}
```

`setAge` — **validation**. Invalid values do not pass.

## Convention

`name` → `getName()` / `setName(...)`. `boolean active` → `isActive()` / `setActive(...)`.

## static

`static` — a field **shared** across the class:

```java
class Counter {
    static int total = 0;
    Counter() { total++; }
}
new Counter();
new Counter();
System.out.println(Counter.total); // 2
```

`Counter.total`, not `p.total`.

## static method

```java
class Math2 {
    static int square(int x) { return x * x; }
}
Math2.square(4);
```

No `new`. Cannot access instance fields.

## What if

- Access `p.age` with `private` — compile error.
- From a `static` method access an instance field — error.
- Forget `static` on a shared counter — each object gets its own.

## Common mistake

Treating `static` as a per-object field. No — it is **class-wide**. Changing it via any instance — visible to all.

## Summary

- `private` — inside the class only
- Getters/setters — controlled access
- `static` field — shared across the class
- `static` method — no object needed
- No `this` in `static`

