17.theory:
## Why

`Animal` with shared fields; `Dog` and `Cat` **inherit** — no code duplication.

## extends

```java
class Animal {
    String name;
    Animal(String name) { this.name = name; }
    void speak() { System.out.println("..."); }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog(String name) { super(name); }
    void wagTail() { System.out.println(name + " wags tail"); }
}
```

`Dog` receives **all** fields and methods of `Animal`.

## super

- `super(args)` — parent constructor. Only on the **first line**.
- `super.method()` — parent method.

If the parent has **no** empty constructor and `super(...)` is not called — compile error.

## What is inherited

Everything except `private` fields and constructors. `private` — only via parent methods.

## Override

```java
class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog(String name) { super(name); }
    @Override
    void speak() { System.out.println(name + ": woof"); }
}
```

`@Override` — check: you really are overriding. Typo in the name — compile error.

## Hierarchy

`Object` — root. `Animal` extends `Object`, `Dog` extends `Animal`.

## What if

- Parent has no empty constructor and `super()` is not called — compile error.
- `private` field in a subclass — inaccessible.
- Override without `@Override` and with a typo — creates a **new** method.

## Common mistake

Forget `@Override`. Typo `speakk` — creates a new method, the parent one is not overridden. `@Override` catches it.

## Summary

- `class B extends A` — inheritance
- `super(...)` — parent constructor
- `@Override` — override check
- `private` is not inherited
- Override changes behavior

