19.theory:
## Abstract class

A parent that **cannot be instantiated**. A template for subclasses.

```java
abstract class Shape {
    abstract double area();
}

class Square extends Shape {
    double side;
    Square(double s) { side = s; }
    @Override double area() { return side * side; }
}

// Shape s = new Shape(); // error
Shape s = new Square(3); // ok
```

`abstract` on a method — "implement yourself". On a class — "cannot instantiate".

## Why

`Shape` without a concrete form is meaningless. Abstraction forces subclasses to **implement** `area()`.

## Interface

A contract. A class can implement **many** interfaces.

```java
interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}

interface Swimmable {
    void swim();
}

class Duck implements Flyable, Swimmable {
    public void fly() { System.out.println("flying"); }
    public void swim() { System.out.println("swimming"); }
}
```

Methods are `public abstract` by default.

## Class vs interface

- **Abstract class** — has fields, constructors, some methods with bodies. Only one parent.
- **Interface** — a method contract. Any number of implementations.
- Need state — abstract class. Need only obligations — interface.

## default in interfaces (Java 8+)

```java
interface Greeter {
    default void hello() { System.out.println("hi"); }
}
```

A body in an interface. Allows adding methods without breaking implementations.

## What if

- `new Shape()` for `abstract` — error.
- A subclass does not implement an abstract method — must be `abstract` itself, or compile error.
- Interface method without `public` in the implementation — error (need `public`).

## Common mistake

Implementation method without `public`. The interface requires `public`. `void area()` instead of `public void area()` — compile error.

## Summary

- `abstract class` — cannot instantiate
- `abstract method` — subclasses must implement
- `interface` — contract
- `implements A, B` — any number
- `default` — method with body in an interface

