22.theory:
## What is an exception

A runtime error. Without handling — the program crashes and prints a **stack trace**:

```
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.ArithmeticException: / by zero
    at Main.main(Main.java:4)
```

## try / catch

```java
try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
}
System.out.println("Continuing");
```

Body in `try`. Exception — in `catch`. Without `catch` — the program crashes.

## finally

```java
try {
    // risky
} catch (Exception e) {
    // handled
} finally {
    System.out.println("always runs");
}
```

`finally` — **always**.

## Typical exceptions

- `ArithmeticException` — division by zero
- `NullPointerException` — working with `null`
- `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` — out of bounds
- `NumberFormatException` — `Integer.parseInt("abc")`
- `InputMismatchException` — `Scanner` gets wrong type

## Multiple catch

```java
try { }
catch (NumberFormatException e) { }
catch (ArithmeticException e) { }
```

First match. More specific first.

## What if

- No `catch` — the program crashes on the exception.
- `catch (Exception e)` catches all — loses specificity.
- Empty `catch { }` — the error is silently eaten; later you cannot figure out what broke.

## Common mistake

Empty `catch` (`catch (Exception e) { }`). All errors are swallowed. At least `e.printStackTrace()` or a message.

## Summary

- `try { } catch (Type e) { }`
- `finally` — always
- Different types — different catch
- `e.getMessage()` — the message
- Do not swallow with empty catch

