30.theory:
## The problem

`ArrayList` without a type stores `Object`. To retrieve — need a cast. Errors at runtime:

```java
ArrayList list = new ArrayList();
list.add("hi");
int x = (int) list.get(0); // ClassCastException at runtime
```

## Solution: `<T>`

```java
ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("hi");
String s = list.get(0); // no cast
```

Type checked by the **compiler**. Errors — before running.

## Custom class

```java
class Box<T> {
    private T value;
    Box(T value) { this.value = value; }
    T get() { return value; }
}

Box<String> b = new Box<>("Hello");
Box<Integer> i = new Box<>(42);
```

`T` — type parameter. `new Box<>()` — type inferred from context.

## Bound `<T extends Number>`

If you need number methods:

```java
class NumericBox<T extends Number> {
    T value;
    NumericBox(T v) { value = v; }
    double asDouble() { return value.doubleValue(); }
}
```

`T extends Number` — only `Integer`, `Double` etc.

## Multiple types

```java
class Pair<K, V> {
    K key; V value;
    Pair(K k, V v) { key = k; value = v; }
}
Pair<String, Integer> p = new Pair<>("a", 1);
```

## No primitives

`Box<int>` — error. Only `Box<Integer>`. Java boxes automatically.

## List.of and Map.of

```java
List<String> list = List.of("a", "b");
Map<String, Integer> map = Map.of("a", 1, "b", 2);
```

Cannot modify.

## What if

- `Box<int>` — error.
- `list.add(42)` in `List<String>` — compile error.
- Forget `<T>` — back to `Object`, manual cast.

## Common mistake

`Box<String> b = new Box("hi");` without `<>` — warning, but works. Write `new Box<>("hi")`.

## Summary

- Generics — type safety
- `<T>` — type parameter
- `<T extends Number>` — bound
- `<K, V>` — multiple
- No primitives — wrappers only
- `List.of` / `Map.of` — factories

