33.theory:
## What enum is

`enum` — enumeration of fixed values.

```java
enum Day { MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI, SAT, SUN }

Day d = Day.MON;
```

Each value is an **object** of class `Day`, not a string.

## Methods

```java
Day[] all = Day.values();          // array of all
Day mon = Day.valueOf("MON");      // from string
String name = mon.name();          // "MON"
int index = mon.ordinal();         // 0
```

## switch with enum

```java
switch (d) {
    case MON:
        System.out.println("Monday");
        break;
    case TUE:
        System.out.println("Tuesday");
        break;
}
```

## Fields and methods

```java
enum Planet {
    MERCURY(3.303e+23, 2.4397e6),
    EARTH(5.976e+24, 6.37814e6);

    private final double mass;
    private final double radius;

    Planet(double mass, double radius) {
        this.mass = mass;
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    double surfaceGravity() {
        return 6.67300E-11 * mass / (radius * radius);
    }
}
```

Each value has its own constructor and fields.

## When to use

- Fixed set: days, order statuses, colors
- Instead of string constants: `"MON"` → `Day.MON`
- In `switch` — readable and safe

## What if

- `Day.valueOf("mon")` — `IllegalArgumentException`, case-sensitive.
- Compare `==` — works, because each value is a singleton.
- `Day.values()` in a hot loop — new array copy each time.

## Common mistake

`switch` without `case` covering all values. Java won't complain but logic breaks. Use `default`.

## Summary

- `enum` — fixed set
- `values()`, `valueOf()`, `name()`, `ordinal()`
- Fields and methods — like a class
- `switch` — cleaner
- `==` — safe to compare

