35.theory:
## The problem

Resources (files, connections) must be **closed**. Forget — leak.

```java
BufferedReader r = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("f.txt"));
try {
    // work
} finally {
    r.close(); // always
}
```

Lots of boilerplate for simple closing.

## try-with-resources

```java
try (BufferedReader r = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("f.txt"))) {
    // work
}
// r closed automatically
```

The resource is declared **in parentheses** after `try`. After the block — closed automatically, even on error.

## AutoCloseable

The resource must implement `AutoCloseable` (or its subinterface `Closeable`):

```java
class Res implements AutoCloseable {
    public void close() {
        System.out.println("closed");
    }
}

try (Res r = new Res()) {
    System.out.println("used");
}
// used
// closed
```

## Multiple resources

```java
try (
    BufferedReader r = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("a.txt"));
    BufferedWriter w = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter("b.txt"))
) {
    // both will close
}
```

Closed in **reverse** order of declaration.

## Comparison

| | try/finally | try-with-resources |
|---|---|---|
| Code | Lots | One line |
| Forget close | Easy | Impossible |
| Exception suppression | No | Yes |

## What if

- Resource is not `AutoCloseable` — compile error.
- `close()` itself throws — added as suppressed to the primary.

## Common mistake

Open the resource **outside** `try`, then use — `close()` won't fire. Always in parens.

## Summary

- `try (resource) { }` — auto-close
- Resource must be `AutoCloseable`
- Multiple via `;`
- Closed in reverse order
- Better than `finally`

